`potion_awkward_gunpowder.json`:
```json
{
  "type": "minecraft:brewing",
  "input": {
	"item": "minecraft:potion",
	"potion_contents": {
	  "potions": "minecraft:awkward"
	}
  },
  "output": {
	"components": {
	  "minecraft:potion_contents": {
		"potion": "minecraft:awkward"
	  }
	},
	"id": "minecraft:splash_potion"
  },
  "reagent": {
	"item": "minecraft:gunpowder"
  }
}
```

Important to note that this doesn't just happen with Awkward potions, I have another mod that uses Poison as a base and it also creates splash and lingering duplicates for Poison as well. This might also happen if you use another mod's potion as a base that isn't your own, but I don't have a setup to test that.

Player's use `EntityPermissionContext`. `EntityPermissionContext#permissionLevel()` is lazily initialised and is only computed once, but this means that if a player's permission level changes the returned permission level is *incorrect*:
```java
@Override
public PermissionLevel permissionLevel() {
	if (this.permissionLevel == null) {
		this.permissionLevel = this.entity instanceof Player player ? CommandPermissionContext.extractPermissionLevel(player.permissions()) : PermissionLevel.ALL;
	}

	return this.permissionLevel;
}
```


You can reproduce this behaviour with the following code:
```java
private int incorrectPermissionBehavior(CommandContext<CommandSourceStack> context) throws CommandSyntaxException {
    var player = context.getSource().getPlayerOrException();
    System.out.println(player.getPermissionContext().permissionLevel());
    context.getSource().getServer().getPlayerList().op(player.nameAndId());
    System.out.println(player.getPermissionContext().permissionLevel());
    return 1;
}
```
Have a player that is not opped join the server, run the above code, observe the output:
```
[STDOUT]: ALL
[STDOUT]: ALL
```
Whereas the expected output is:
```
[STDOUT]: ALL
[STDOUT]: OWNER
```

