The way the Client GameTest API currently takes screenshots in 26.1.2 is incompatible with Sodium 0.9.1-beta.3, crashing the game with:
```
java.lang.IllegalStateException: Global terrain uniforms have not been updated
    at UniformBufferManager.getUniformBuffer(UniformBufferManager.java:109)
    ...
    at ClientGameTestContextImpl.doTakeScreenshot(ClientGameTestContextImpl.java:398)
```

This crash is triggered by <https://github.com/CaffeineMC/sodium/pull/3788>, which basically asserts that the vanilla `GameRenderer.update()` -> `LevelRenderer.update()` -> `LevelRenderer.cullTerrain()` chain always happens before `GameRenderer.extract()`.

This assertion holds in vanilla, but Fabric's `ClientGameTestContextImpl.doTakeScreenshot()` renders a frame by calling only:
```java
client.gameRenderer.extract(deltaTracker, true);
client.gameRenderer.render(deltaTracker, true);
```

Compared to vanilla `Minecraft.grabPanoramixScreenshot()` (which iirc `doTakeScreenshot()` was originally based on):
```java
gameRenderer.update(DeltaTracker.ONE, true);
gameRenderer.extract(DeltaTracker.ONE, true);
gameRenderer.renderLevel(DeltaTracker.ONE);
```

One could argue Sodium shouldn't have made that assertion and/or should have relied on something that gets called from `GameRenderer.extract()` instead (as they did in 26.2, hence why it doesn't crash there), but this would be a one-line fix in `ClientGameTestContextImpl.doTakeScreenshot()`:

```diff
+client.gameRenderer.update(deltaTracker, true);
client.gameRenderer.extract(deltaTracker, true);
client.gameRenderer.render(deltaTracker, true);
```

In FAPI 0.148.0+26.1.2, you could safely add a `TagProvider`  such as `MyTagProvider extends HolderTagProvider<Potion>` to a pack. Starting sometime at or before 0.149.1, doing so will crash datagen due to a cast in a mixin. Here's the [diff](https://github.com/FabricMC/fabric-api/compare/0.148.0+26.1.2...0.151.0+26.1.2#diff-35640ba77b05325d865d0383902d0dfc6bc5b54f72bf253b5a3393b9ca3a7e55R74-R78) with the change highlighted, though GitHub doesn't show it well, so here's the [line](https://github.com/FabricMC/fabric-api/blame/f6a20d9b6798132d6556f0ab4f5cc46333ece3da/fabric-data-generation-api-v1/src/main/java/net/fabricmc/fabric/mixin/datagen/TagsProviderMixin.java#L76) and [commit](https://github.com/FabricMC/fabric-api/commit/73b1f905050a05f3585ce65971502e68f589c4be).

Workaround:
Implement `TagsProvider<Potion>` instead of `HolderTagProvider<Potion>` and add a helper:
```java
protected TagAppender<Holder.Reference<Potion>, Potion> tag(TagKey<Potion> tag) {
        return TagAppender.<Potion>forBuilder(getOrCreateRawBuilder(tag)).map(Holder.Reference::key);
}
```

Possible solutions:
1. Remove the cast (I don't know enough fapi internals to know if this is easy/hard)
2. Specify in `FabricTagsProvider` javadoc that vanilla implementations are not supported (easiest?)
3. Print a more specific error message on a failed cast (pairs well with 2)


