I have been playing with the recently added API for custom `EntityFluidInteraction`s. I've registered a fluid swimming behaviour for a custom fluid and it all works nicely, except that whenever I am standing on the ground in the fluid and hold the space bar, I don't swim up, but jump directly from the ground as if I wasn't in a fluid.

It happens in this bit of code in `LivingEntity.aiStep`:
```java
if (!inWaterAndHasFluidHeight || this.onGround() && !(fluidHeight > fluidJumpThreshold)) {
        // specifically here in this if condition
	if (!this.isInLava() || this.onGround() && this.isInShallowFluid(FluidTags.LAVA)) {
		if ((this.onGround() || inWaterAndHasFluidHeight && fluidHeight <= fluidJumpThreshold) && this.noJumpDelay == 0) {
			this.jumpFromGround();
			this.noJumpDelay = 10;
		}
	} else {
		this.jumpInLiquid(FluidTags.LAVA);
	}
} else {
	this.jumpInLiquid(FluidTags.WATER);
}
```

The API has a mixin hook at `!isInLava()` to see if the entity is in a custom fluid, supposedly this was intended to avoid jumping when the entity is in a custom fluid. However, there follows the condition `onGround() && isInShallowFluid(FluidTags.LAVA)`, which is considered true since the player is, technically, in shallow lava (anything not lava is considered shallow as it has height 0 of that fluid). This causes the condition to be true regardless of the custom fluid being there, since the custom fluid is not lava.

If we look into the `LivingEntityMixin` used here, we find this hook for `isInLava()`
```java
@ModifyExpressionValue(
    method = "aiStep",
    at = @At(
        value = "INVOKE",
        target = "Lnet/minecraft/world/entity/LivingEntity;isInLava()Z"
    ),
    slice = @Slice(from = @At(
        value = "INVOKE",
        target = "Lnet/minecraft/world/entity/LivingEntity;getFluidJumpThreshold()D"
    ))
)
private boolean jumpInCustomFluid(boolean original, @Share("fluid") LocalRef<TagKey<Fluid>> fluid) {
    return original || fluid.get() != null;
}
```

This creates the following code there:
```java
if (!inWaterAndHasFluidHeight || this.onGround() && !(fluidHeight > fluidJumpThreshold)) {
        // specifically here in this if condition
	if (!jumpInCustomFluid(this.isInLava(), fluidRef) || this.onGround() && this.isInShallowFluid(FluidTags.LAVA)) {
		// jumping from ground...
                // this still happens, because isInShallowFluid(LAVA) is still true
	} else {
		// swimming in custom fluid...
	}
} else {
	// swimming in water...
}
```

To fix the bug this there should be a second hook there:
```java
// forgive me for any errors I typed this directly into the issue

@Shadow
boolean isInShallowFluid(TagKey<Fluid> fluidTag);

@ModifyExpressionValue(
    method = "aiStep",
    at = @At(
        value = "INVOKE", // target isInShallowFluid
        target = "Lnet/minecraft/world/entity/LivingEntity;isInShallowFluid(Lnet/minecraft/tags/TagKey;)Z"
    ),
    slice = @Slice(from = @At(
        value = "INVOKE",
        target = "Lnet/minecraft/world/entity/LivingEntity;getFluidJumpThreshold()D"
    ))
)
private boolean jumpInCustomFluid2(boolean original, @Share("fluid") LocalRef<TagKey<Fluid>> fluid) {
    // inject the custom fluid tag here instead of FluidTags.LAVA
    // so that the jumping doesn't trigger unless the fluid is shallow

    // this could potentially be extended with extra logic in FluidBehavior
    // to allow custom jump thresholds
    TagKey<Fluid> customFluid = fluid.get();
    return customFluid != null ? isInShallowFluid(customFluid) : original;
}
```

This then creates the following code:
```java
if (!inWaterAndHasFluidHeight || this.onGround() && !(fluidHeight > fluidJumpThreshold)) {
        // specifically here in this if condition
	if (!jumpInCustomFluid(this.isInLava(), fluidRef) || this.onGround() && jumpInCustomFluid2(this.isInShallowFluid(FluidTags.LAVA), fluidRef)) {
		// jumping from ground...
                // no longer happens as mixin injects custom fluid tag now
	} else {
		// swimming in custom fluid...
	}
} else {
	// swimming in water...
}
```

Glad to see #5365 get merged, I think I may have found a bug in it though. When trying to remove a tag from another tag, it sometimes does not work, I believe because the dependency sorting doesn't take the removal entries into account.

## Steps to reproduce

Create a mod with the following mod initializer and tags

```java
